---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="09-recursion"
  lessonSlug="01-factorial"
  hook="The most-quoted recursion in computer science. Two-line body, induction-style correctness."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def factorial(n):
    if n == 0:
        return 1                       # base case
    return n * factorial(n - 1)        # recursive case

for x in [0, 1, 5, 6, 10]:
    print(f"factorial({x}) = {factorial(x)}")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="The leap of faith (TP §6.7)">
    <p>
      Don't trace the calls. Ask two questions:<br/>
      <strong>1.</strong> What's the smallest case I can solve directly? <code>factorial(0) == 1</code>.<br/>
      <strong>2.</strong> If I had <code>factorial(n-1)</code>, how do I get <code>factorial(n)</code>? Multiply by n.<br/>
      Done. Function correct by induction.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Trace once, for understanding</h2>
  <pre class="text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>factorial(3)        → 3 * factorial(2)
  factorial(2)      → 2 * factorial(1)
    factorial(1)    → 1 * factorial(0)
      factorial(0)  → 1                          ← BASE
    factorial(1)    → 1 * 1 = 1
  factorial(2)      → 2 * 1 = 2
factorial(3)        → 3 * 2 = 6</code></pre>

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="No loops — that's a different lesson">
    <p>
      The autograder rejects <code>for</code> and <code>while</code>. An
      iterative <code>factorial</code> is a perfectly fine Ch.3 exercise
      — but here we're building the recursion muscle.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
